PART A — minimal unit-distance path realizing a norm-t (=dist sqrt(t)) pair
  (Eisenstein word length = min # unit steps; #points = length+1)
    t  sqrt(t)  Loeschian  min#pts min#edges
    1    1.000        yes        2         1
    3    1.732        yes        3         2
    4    2.000        yes        3         2
    7    2.646        yes        4         3
    9    3.000        yes        4         3
   12    3.464        yes        5         4
   13    3.606        yes        5         4
   16    4.000        yes        5         4
   19    4.359        yes        6         5
   21    4.583        yes        6         5
   25    5.000        yes        6         5
   27    5.196        yes        7         6
   28    5.292        yes        7         6
   31    5.568        yes        7         6
  => the MINIMAL transverse distance >1 is sqrt(3) (t=3), realized by a 3-point
     2-edge path; t=4 needs a colinear length-2 (3 pts collinear); larger t need
     progressively larger factors. The optimal 3-pt UDG is K3 (sqrt3-FREE), which
     is why 27=3^3 gets zero bonus but 28=4*7 (rhombus has a sqrt3 pair) crosses.

DONE.
